"></a><div class="caseBody"><div class="caseType">Independent product</div><h3>ABIQ Sports Decision Intelligence</h3><p>An end-to-end sports decision platform combining predictive models, automated data workflows, model monitoring, and user-facing recommendations.</p><div class="skillsLine">Product ownership · Predictive modeling · Automation</div><div class="caseActions"><a class="button primary" href="https://abiq-sports.streamlit.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open ABIQ</a><a class="button" href="#abiq-story">View case study</a></div></div></article>
</div></div></section>
<section class="section white" id="stories"><div class="shell"><div class="sectionHead"><div><h2>Case studies</h2><p>The finished interfaces are only part of the story. These projects also show how I frame the problem, translate business logic into product behavior, and refine the workflow around the user.</p></div></div><div class="storyList">
<article class="story" id="field-story"><div><div class="storyNo">Case Study 01</div><h3>Field Sales Portfolio Optimization</h3><p class="storyLead">Turn a high-constraint territory planning process into an interactive workspace where leaders can understand tradeoffs before committing to a change.</p></div><div class="storyDetails"><div class="detail"><b>Problem</b><p>Leaders had to balance revenue, account capacity, geography, continuity, and operating rules at the same time. Static outputs made tradeoffs difficult to evaluate.</p></div><div class="detail"><b>Product decisions</b><p>Protect the baseline, expose constraints, separate hierarchy review from scenario work, and make account movement traceable through the workflow.</p></div><div class="detail"><b>My role</b><p>Problem framing, business-rule translation, analytical logic, interaction design, prototyping, scenario behavior, and stakeholder refinement.</p></div></div></article>
<article class="story" id="market-story"><div><div class="storyNo">Case Study 02</div><h3>Market Opportunity Navigator</h3><p class="storyLead">Connect market intelligence, relationship context, and seller ownership so different users can move from opportunity signal to an executable next action.</p></div><div class="storyDetails"><div class="detail"><b>Problem</b><p>Market opportunity, relationship coverage, seller ownership, and future activity were visible in separate analytical views but were difficult to translate into action.</p></div><div class="detail"><b>Product decisions</b><p>Organize the experience around user jobs, create distinct executive and seller entry points, connect market signals to relationships, and make target lists exportable.</p></div><div class="detail"><b>My role</b><p>Experience concept, workflow definition, metric design, drilldown logic, interaction refinement, visual design, and action-oriented storytelling.</p></div></div></article>
<article class="story" id="abiq-story"><div><div class="storyNo">Case Study 03</div><h3>ABIQ Sports Decision Intelligence</h3><p class="storyLead">Build an independent decision platform where predictive modeling is one layer of a broader product that supports strategy, risk, and repeated weekly decisions.</p></div><div class="storyDetails"><div class="detail"><b>Problem</b><p>Raw probabilities are not enough for repeated survivor decisions. Users also need future value, uncertainty, pool behavior, and multi-entry risk considered together.</p></div><div class="detail"><b>Product decisions</b><p>Separate prediction from strategy optimization, expose model performance, automate refreshes, preserve user state, and translate model output into recommendations.</p></div><div class="detail"><b>My role</b><p>Product definition, data engineering, modeling, validation, optimization design, automation, UI design, deployment, and documentation.</p></div></div></article>
</div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="approach"><div class="shell"><div class="sectionHead"><div><h2>How I work</h2><p>The common thread is translating ambiguous business logic into a clear experience that supports a real decision.</p></div></div><div class="processGrid"><div class="processItem"><div class="n">01</div><h3>Frame the decision</h3><p>Clarify the user, the difficult decision, and what a useful outcome actually looks like.</p></div><div class="processItem"><div class="n">02</div><h3>Translate the rules</h3><p>Turn operating constraints, data logic, and edge cases into product behavior.</p></div><div class="processItem"><div class="n">03</div><h3>Prototype the workflow</h3><p>Build working interactions early enough to expose gaps and test the experience.</p></div><div class="processItem"><div class="n">04</div><h3>Refine for action</h3><p>Use feedback to simplify the flow and connect insight to the next operational step.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section white" id="about"><div class="shell"><div class="about"><div><h2>Analytics depth with a product mindset.</h2></div><div><p>My background is in analytics, strategy, and operations, but the work I gravitate toward sits between analysis and product. I like defining what a tool needs to do, translating business requirements into interaction logic, and refining the experience until the answer is clear enough for someone to act on.</p><p>I have built decision-support products for territory planning, market opportunity, seller workflows, predictive modeling, and sports strategy, using a mix of SQL, Python, HTML, Salesforce, BI tools, and automation.</p><div class="note"><b>Portfolio note:</b> Enterprise demos use sample data. Sales, opportunity, customer, market, seller, and related business values are fictionalized for demonstration while preserving the interaction design and workflow of the original products.</div></div></div></div></section>
<footer class="footer"><div class="shell">Abigail Millsap · Product Strategy · Decision Intelligence · Analytics Products</div></footer>
